<%# The Leistungsstufe switcher, rendered only INSIDE the card the org is on
    (KI-Pakete P3, doc/ki_pakete_plan.md §7). Two axes, two places: the
    jurisdiction is chosen by picking a card, the rung is chosen in it — and §2
    is emphatic that the second must not look like the first, because only the
    first carries legal weight and a reindex.

    It posts to the SAME select_package action with the org's own package id and
    a different tier_key. One write, one protocol trail (§7), and the Actor is
    what knows a rung change may skip the embedding guard — a second endpoint
    would be a second place to get that right.

    Local: package (the org's currently selected one). %>
<% current_key = @organisation.ai_package_tier_key.presence || AiPackageTier::DEFAULT_KEY %>

<div class="mt-2 flex flex-wrap gap-2" data-tier-switch="<%= package.id %>">
  <% package.tiers.each do |tier| %>
    <% active = tier.key == current_key %>
    <%# A rung whose credential cannot serve chat — disabled, wrong purpose or no
        endpoint (usable_for?, the same three conditions the Factory refuses on) —
        is refused by the Actor too (:tier_credential_unusable). Offering it would
        be a button whose only outcome is an error message. %>
    <% usable = tier.chat_credential&.usable_for?(:chat) %>
    <%# ...and a SECOND reason a rung may not be offered, added with P4c (E5): the
        package itself was withdrawn. Actors::SelectAiPackage#resolve_package answers
        :package_not_published on EVERY write, rung changes included — the P3
        relaxation for tier-only writes was deliberately dropped because it tore a
        hole in the ladder validation. Before E5 this partial never met such a
        package (the grid renders from the `published` scope), so the gap was
        unreachable; E5 puts the withdrawn package's own card on screen and makes it
        reachable in one step.

        Two reasons, two texts, on purpose. A withdrawn package's rungs are not
        BROKEN — the chat behind them works, the org is running on one of them — so
        labelling them "nicht verfügbar" would name the wrong fault and send the
        customer looking at credentials instead of at the package.

        RANK, for the case where both are true (P4c review, I4): WITHDRAWN WINS. With
        the package withdrawn no rung is reachable whatever its credential says, so
        naming the credential would be the misleading half of the sentence above.
        Nothing is hidden by that choice — the ACTIVE rung's chip carries its own
        #usable_for? warning independently, so a broken credential still shows up
        where the org actually runs. %>
    <% offerable = usable && package.published? %>
    <%# Which provider and model this rung actually talks to — the same question
        the card answers for the package as a whole, one level down. Computed
        before the branch so every rendering says the identical thing. %>
    <% detail = [tier.chat_credential&.display_label, tier.effective_chat_model].compact_blank.join(' · ').presence ||
                t('views.organisations.ai_settings.packages.card.tier_provider_missing') %>
    <%# The active rung is a STATE, not an action: as a button it would invite a
        click that re-posts the rung the org is already on and changes nothing
        visible. Same shape, no form. %>
    <% if active %>
      <%# "Gewählt" AND broken is a real state, and the honest rendering shows
          both: the org IS on this rung (that is what its column says), and the
          rung cannot currently serve. Deciding the branch on `active` alone
          painted a disabled credential as healthy precisely where it hurts most —
          on the rung the org's chat actually runs on. %>
      <span class="inline-flex flex-col items-start px-3 py-2 rounded border-2 border-ink bg-ink text-cream-50"
            data-tier-option="<%= tier.key %>" data-tier-active="true"
            <%= 'data-tier-disabled="true"'.html_safe unless usable %>>
        <span class="text-sm font-bold">
          <i class="bi bi-check-lg mr-1"></i><%= tier.label %>
        </span>
        <span class="text-xs opacity-90"><%= detail %></span>
        <% unless usable %>
          <span class="text-xs font-bold">
            <i class="bi bi-exclamation-triangle mr-1"></i><%= t('views.organisations.ai_settings.packages.card.tier_unavailable') %>
          </span>
        <% end %>
      </span>
    <% elsif !offerable %>
      <%# Shown rather than hidden: the rung is part of what the package offers,
          and a silently missing option reads as "this package has one stufe". %>
      <span class="inline-flex flex-col items-start px-3 py-2 rounded border border-dashed border-cream-300 bg-cream-100 opacity-60 cursor-not-allowed"
            data-tier-option="<%= tier.key %>" data-tier-disabled="true">
        <span class="text-sm font-bold text-cream-700">
          <i class="bi bi-lock mr-1"></i><%= tier.label %>
        </span>
        <span class="text-xs text-cream-700">
          <%= t("views.organisations.ai_settings.packages.card.#{package.published? ? 'tier_unavailable' : 'tier_withdrawn'}") %>
        </span>
      </span>
    <% else %>
      <%= button_to select_package_organisation_ai_settings_path(@organisation),
                    method: :post,
                    params: { ai_package_id: package.id, ai_package_tier_key: tier.key },
                    class: 'inline-flex flex-col items-start px-3 py-2 rounded border border-cream-300 ' \
                           'bg-surface-raised text-left hover:bg-cream-100 hover:border-ink transition-colors',
                    data: { tier_option: tier.key } do %>
        <span class="text-sm font-bold text-cream-900"><%= tier.label %></span>
        <span class="text-xs text-cream-700"><%= detail %></span>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</div>

<%# §2.1 made visible: the rung the customer picks does NOT govern the helper
    paths, and a customer who pays for "Gründlich" and then reads a terse ticket
    summary would otherwise conclude the switch is broken. Naming the affected
    features beats naming the rule.

    The rung is named by AiPackage#utility_tier — the SAME call the routing makes
    (Factory#curated_tier). Deriving it here would let the screen promise a rung
    the request does not use, which is the one way this sentence could become a
    lie.

    …and NAMING it is only half of not lying. The utility rung is judged for
    usability by the same #usable_for?(:chat) as every chip above (the routing
    refuses on all three of its conditions, Factory#curated_chat_resolution), so
    the state "org sits on Gründlich, operator disabled the fast credential"
    cannot render the sentence "helper tasks always run on Schnell" while all
    five of them are in fact down. That state is reachable without touching the
    package: AiPackageTier validates its credential on write, never after. %>
<% utility_tier = package.utility_tier %>
<% if utility_tier %>
  <% utility_usable = utility_tier.chat_credential&.usable_for?(:chat) %>
  <p class="mt-3 text-xs <%= utility_usable ? 'text-cream-700' : 'font-bold text-red-800' %>">
    <i class="bi <%= utility_usable ? 'bi-info-circle' : 'bi-exclamation-triangle' %> mr-1"></i>
    <%= t("views.organisations.ai_settings.packages.card.#{utility_usable ? 'tier_utility_hint' : 'tier_utility_unavailable'}",
          tier: utility_tier.label) %>
  </p>
<% end %>
